alidity: The solution uses valid methods: combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, counting arguments, construction. They define L and R, prove a claim that no tile can cross L and R. That is a valid reasoning using geometry of rectangles and the permutation matrix. Then they prove each rectangle in L can contain at most one of the rightmost cells r_i. That's a valid argument using rectangle shape and the uncovered squares. Similarly for R. So the lower bound is valid. The construction is explicit: they give T_j and S_j definitions, which are concrete details (vertical strips). So they provide concrete specification for each tile. This satisfies Category C for Level 2. So reasoning uses valid methods.\n\nThus Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps? Let's examine the solution for any missing details or imprecise wording.\n\n- The claim that no tile can contain cells from both L and R is proven. The proof uses the fact that the rectangle's column interval contains both j1 and j2, thus contains sigma(i). Then they say \"Since the row interval of Q contains row i (it contains (i,j1)), the cell (i, sigma(i)) lies inside Q, contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.\" This is correct.\n\n- Lemma 1: They define r_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) as the rightmost cell of that row belonging to L. They claim any rectangle in L contains at most one such cell. The proof: Suppose rectangle Q contains two distinct rightmost cells r_i1 and r_i2 with c1 < c2. Since Q is rectangle, its rightmost column is at least c2, thus Q also contains cell (i1, c2). Since sigma(i1) = c1+1 <= c2, the cell (i1,c2) is uncovered square of row i1, which cannot belong to L. Contradiction. This is correct.\n\n- They then note exactly one row satisfies sigma(i)=1, all other n-1 rows have sigma(i)>1. So set {r_i} contains n-1 distinct cells. This is correct.\n\n- Similarly for Lemma 2.\n\n- Lower bound: sum of lower bounds for L and R yields 2n-2.\