.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement can be encoded by an \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij}) ,\\qquad \na_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if the unit square }(i,j)\\text{ is covered},\\\\[2mm]\n0 &\\text{if it is the unique uncovered square of its row and column}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one zero, after a suitable permutation of the columns we may assume that the zeros lie on the main diagonal; i.e.  \n\n\\[\na_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }i=j,\\\\\n1 &\\text{if }i\\neq j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nA = J-I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An explicit tiling with \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) place a single rectangular tile that occupies all squares of that row except the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\).  \nFormally, the tile for row \\(i\\) is the set  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle.  \nThe tiles \\(T_1,T_2,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct rows) and together cover precisely the entries equal to \\(1\\) in the matrix (1).  \nHence \\(n\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound via matrix rank  \n\nEach rectangular tile \\(R\\) covering rows \\(R_{\\text{row}}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(R_{\\text{col}}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) corresponds to the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nM_R = u_R v_R^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n(u_R)_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }i\\in R_{\\text{row}},\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n(v_R)_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }j\\in R_{\\text{col}},\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile may not contain a diagonal square, we must have  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\text{row}}\\cap R_{\\text{col}}=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(M_R\\) has rank \\(1\\) (or rank \\(0\\) if the tile is empty).  \n\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(k\\) tiles \\(R^{(1)},\\dots ,R^{(k)}\\).  \nSince the tiles are disjoint, the matrix \\(A\\) of covered squares is the (ordinary) sum of the matrices of the tiles:\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_{R^{(t)}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe rank of a sum of matrices does not exceed the sum of their ranks; therefore\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{R^{(t)}})\\le k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).  \nThe matrix \\(J\\) has rank \\(1\\); the identity \\(I\\) has rank \\(n\\).  \nSince \\(J\\) and \\(I\\) are linearly independent,  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n .\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, the eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with multiplicity \\(n-1\\).)  \n\nCombining this with (4) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling that uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles and proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n(For the trivial case \\(n=1\\) the board consists of a single square, which must be left uncovered; consequently zero tiles are needed, consistent with the formula.)"
