ction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=2\\)\n\nThe complete digraph on two vertices has the two arcs \\(1\\to2\\) and \\(2\\to1\\).\nA single cut can contain at most one of them (a cut contains arcs only from its\nsource side to its sink side).  Hence at least two cuts are required, and\n\\(2\\cdot2-2=2\\) indeed holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement true for size \\(n-1\\) (with \\(n\\ge3\\)).\nLet \\(\\mathcal C=\\{(A_k,B_k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,r\\}\\) be a family of cuts that\ncovers all arcs of the complete digraph on the vertex set  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause vertex \\(n\\) has outgoing arcs to every other vertex, there exists a\ncut in which \\(n\\) belongs to the source side; choose one and denote it\n\\(C^{\\text{out}}=(A^{\\text{out}},B^{\\text{out}})\\) with \\(n\\in A^{\\text{out}}\\).\nAnalogously, because vertex \\(n\\) has incoming arcs from every other vertex,\nthere exists a cut \\(C^{\\text{in}}=(A^{\\text{in}},B^{\\text{in}})\\) with\n\\(n\\in B^{\\text{in}}\\).  \nSince a cut never contains the same vertex on both sides, the two cuts are\ndistinct.\n\nIf \\(A^{\\text{out}}\\) contains a vertex different from \\(n\\), replace\n\\(C^{\\text{out}}\\) by the **pure** cut  \n\n\\[\n\\widehat C^{\\text{out}}=(\\{n\\},V\\setminus\\{n\\}).\n\\]\n\nAll arcs that start at \\(n\\) are still covered (they were covered by\n\\(C^{\\text{out}}\\) and the new cut contains the same sink side), and no new\narc is introduced because the source side has become smaller.  The same\nreplacement can be performed for \\(C^{\\text{in}}\\), yielding the pure cut  \n\n\\[\n\\widehat C^{\\text{in}}=(V\\setminus\\{n\\},\\{n\\}).\n\\]\n\nThus we may assume that the family \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains the two pure cuts\n\\(\\widehat C^{\\text{out}}\\) and \\(\\widehat C^{\\text{in}}\\).\n\nNow delete the vertex \\(n\\) from the digraph and delete the two pure cuts.\nFor every ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i,j\\in V\\setminus\\{n\\}\\) the original\nfamily contained a cut covering this arc; such a cut cannot involve the vertex\n\\(n\\) (otherwise it would have placed \\(i\\) on the source side and \\(j\\) on the\nsink side while also containing \\(n\\) on one of the sides, which would not\naffect the covering of \\((i,j)\\)).  Hence the remaining cuts still cover all\narcs of the complete digraph on the vertex set  \n\n\\[\nV' = V\\setminus\\{n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(n-1\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, any covering of this \\((n-1)\\)-vertex digraph\nneeds at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)-2\n\\]\n\ncuts.  Adding back the two pure cuts that involve the vertex \\(n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nr\\;\\ge\\; \\bigl(2(n-1)-2\\bigr)+2 \\;=\\; 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus any family of cuts covering all arcs of the complete digraph without loops\ncontains at least \\(2n-2\\) cuts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Translation back to tiles  \n\nEach cut corresponds to a rectangular tile, and the covering condition for cuts\nis exactly the requirement that every off\u2011diagonal square of the board be\ncovered by at least one tile.  Consequently any tiling of the board satisfying\nMatilda\u2019s condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with \\(2n-2=4048\\) tiles and proved that no tiling\ncan use fewer tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
