nt of \\(S_L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\) contained two distinct squares \\((i_1,c_1)\\) and \\((i_2,c_2)\\) of \\(S_L\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is axis‑parallel, its column interval contains all columns from \\(c_1\\) to \\(c_2\\), in particular column \\(c_1+1=\\pi(i_1)\\). Because row \\(i_1\\) belongs to \\(T\\), the square \\((i_1,\\pi(i_1))\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), contradicting Lemma 1. ∎\n\nThus each tile covering \\(L\\) can cover at most one element of \\(S_L\\), and because \\(|S_L|=n-1\\) we must have \\(t_L\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(R\\))\n\nDefine analogously  \n\\[\nS_R:=\\{(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\nAgain \\(|S_R|=n-1\\). By the same argument as in Lemma 2 (now using the squares immediately to the right of each hole) a rectangle lying in \\(R\\) can contain at most one element of \\(S_R\\). Hence \\(t_R\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board\n\nFrom Lemmas 2 and 3,\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles }=t_L+t_R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles for the region above the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile\n\\[\nH_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich has size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n*Tiles for the region below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile\n\\[\nV_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square of \\(B\\) except the diagonal holes, and their total number is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Optimality\n\nEquation (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while (2) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:** The minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. All mathematical statements are expressed in TeX delimiters.\n2. Lemma 2 and Lemma 3 correctly argue that each rectangle can contain at most one of the designated “adjacent‑to‑hole” squares, using the column‑interval argument.\n3. The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) follows directly from Lemmas 2 and 3.\n4. The construction with diagonal holes indeed covers every non‑hole square exactly once and uses precisely \\((n-1)+(n-1)\\) tiles.\n5. The final numeric computation for \\(n=2025\\) yields \\(4048\\).\n6. The output follows the required section headings and format precisely."
