]\n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells of column \\(j\\) **above** the diagonal:\n     \\[\n     S_j=\\{(i,j): i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{S_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, each is a legitimate tile, and together they cover every cell except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n   Thus \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   The answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nDefine the two regions  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of the cells strictly to the left of the empty square in each row, and \\(R\\) consists of the cells strictly to the right.\n\n**Claim.** No tile (axis\u2011parallel rectangle) can contain cells from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains a cell \\((i,j_1)\\in L\\) and a cell \\((k,j_2)\\in R\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, its column interval \\([c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]\\) contains both \\(j_1\\) and \\(j_2\\); hence it also contains every integer column between them, in particular the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nSince the row interval of \\(Q\\) contains row \\(i\\) (it contains \\((i,j_1)\\)), the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) lies inside \\(Q\\), contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares. \u220e\n\nThus every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (left region)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\n\\]\n\nis the rightmost cell of that row belonging to \\(L\\).  \nAny rectangle contained in \\(L\\) contains at most one of the cells \\(\\{r_i\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.* Assume a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct rightmost cells \\(r_{i_1}=(i_1,c_1)\\) and \\(r_{i_2}=(i_2,c_2)\\) with \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, its rightmost column is at least \\(c_2\\); therefore \\(Q\\) also contains the cell \\((i_1,c_2)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(i_1)=c_1+1\\le c_2\\), the cell \\((i_1,c_2)\\) is precisely the uncovered square of row \\(i_1\\), which cannot belong to \\(L\\).  \nThis contradiction shows that \\(Q\\) cannot contain two distinct rightmost cells. \u220e\n\nExactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\).  \nHence the set \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) distinct cells, and by Lemma\u202f1 each rectangle of a tiling of \\(L\\) can cover at most one of them.  \nConsequently any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (right region)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost cell of that row belonging to \\(R\\).  \nA symmetric argument to Lemma\u202f1 shows that any rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one of the cells \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\).  \nSince exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\), there are again \\(n-1\\) such cells, so any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); therefore the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle consisting of the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **below** the diagonal.  \nThe rectangles \\(T_1,T_2,\\dots ,T_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Tiles right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_j\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle consisting of the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie **above** the diagonal.  \nThe rectangles \\(S_2,S_3,\\dots ,S_n\\) are also pairwise disjoint and are disjoint from all \\(T_j\\) because they occupy complementary rows.\n\nEvery cell of the board that is not on the diagonal belongs either to some \\(T_j\\) (if it is below the diagonal) or to some \\(S_j\\) (if it is above the diagonal).  \nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{T_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{S_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\n\\]\n\ncovers all non\u2011diagonal cells, uses exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles, and satisfies the condition that each row and column has a single uncovered square (the diagonal square).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nThe tiling just described uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, matching the lower bound (1).  \nHence the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\bo